#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc8b157-7c31-5520-86d5-398490e4cf98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294079} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b09eeb-837b-5d5d-8fc4-0f380227af07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294080} {:answer/id #uuid "62d0cf2f-a834-5fcc-ac44-2090d7ab89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294081} {:answer/id #uuid "13fb1a98-3b74-5fc9-94b5-677f6f9553db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294082} {:answer/id #uuid "48c2fa81-7f35-5e0a-955d-625d74477f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d0cf2f-a834-5fcc-ac44-2090d7ab89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294081} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a6ffea-ff76-537a-8399-e287f6d1cecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fbbf821-90cb-5820-99c9-f84523b8d2c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79200" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/012c84c1dfaeb5802adbecea30c3c184a13b6802f2509e5983f6b3682fe39f79.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$"}]}
